#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""upgrade_cost: what it costs one character to get an item to a quality at the Forge of Potential, the honest way.

No name tricks and no relay: one character crafts the item, upgrades it at its crafting station as far as the station
allows, then upgrades it at the Forge of Potential. When an attempt fails, the item breaks; the character crafts a new
copy and starts over. Every roll is the character's real roll, replayed from its name and the seeds stored in its file
(see upgrade_oracle.py), so the result is exact for that character, not an average.

    python3 upgrade_cost.py --item "Iron Sword" --target 10 --name Ragnar
    python3 upgrade_cost.py --item "Iron Sword" --target 10 --save Ragnar.fch     # uses the seeds already stored
    python3 upgrade_cost.py --item "Iron Sword" --target 10 --name Ragnar -v      # every copy, step by step

Costs, as the game computes them (Piece.Requirement.GetAmount, Player.ConsumeResources, InventoryGui.DoCrafting):
  * Crafting takes each ingredient's amount. Deep North gear and staffs are crafted as a cast instead, which the Frost
    Foundry cooks into the item on Liquid Frost (5 per cast); a copy then costs the cast recipe plus that fuel.
  * Upgrading to quality q at the station takes per-level amounts:
    (q - 1) x per level below quality 4, and (4 + (q - 4) / 2) x per level (rounded down) from quality 4 on.
  * The station only reaches a level of 1 + one per kind of extension, so some items (Deep North gear, for example)
    stop below their max quality there. The copy is taken to that cap before it goes to the Forge.
  * Each Forge attempt costs one Idol of the item's tier, success or failure; the station ingredients are not used.
  * Gear used as an ingredient (the Nord Sword in a Frostfire Sword, the Ripper in a Wound Ripper) is counted as what
    it takes to craft, recursively, and is never refunded.
  * A break refunds part of the refundable ingredients of the item's upgrade recipe: BREAK_RETURN x (craft amount +
    the amount of the last level reached), rounded up. Idols and foundry fuel are never refunded, and neither is a cast
    ingredient the upgrade recipe leaves out (the staffs' moulds).

Needs upgrade_oracle.py next to it (for reading saves and replaying rolls). Python 3, standard library only.
"""
import argparse
import json
import math
import sys
from collections import Counter

import upgrade_oracle as oracle

MAX_COPIES = 1_000_000          # give up after crafting this many copies

# BEGIN GENERATED COST DATA (tools/update_cost_data.py rewrites everything up to END GENERATED COST DATA)
